description:
email.rs:104       email_set(create: Option<...>, update: Option<...>, destroy: Option<...>, if_in_state: Option<&State>)
mailbox.rs:82      mailbox_set(create, update, destroy, params)
identity.rs:77     identity_set(create, update, destroy)
submission.rs:160  email_submission_set(create, update, destroy, if_in_state, params)
vacation.rs:54     vacation_response_set(update)   ← only takes update, less acute

A caller can invoke `email_set(None, None, None, None)` and the function will dutifully serialize accountId only, send it to the server, and return a SetResponse that does nothing. This is a wasted HTTP round-trip and a wasted server-side state increment (the server still updates state because /set always advances state per RFC 8620 §5.3, even when no operations are requested — though spec-conformant servers MAY no-op).

Compare with email_import (line 255) which DOES reject empty input:

  if emails.is_empty() {
      return Err(jmap_base_client::ClientError::InvalidArgument(
          "email_import: emails map may not be empty".into(),
      ));
  }

…and email_parse (line 304) which rejects empty blob_ids. The same defensive guard is missing on the four /set methods.

The "all-None /set" footgun is real: a caller writing a generic /set helper that constructs create/update/destroy from optional inputs may pass all-None and get a no-op round-trip instead of a compile error or runtime rejection. Bug-hiding.

Recommendation: add a defensive guard at the top of each /set method:

  if create.is_none() && update.is_none() && destroy.is_none() {
      return Err(jmap_base_client::ClientError::InvalidArgument(
          "<method>: at least one of create, update, destroy must be Some".into(),
      ));
  }

Same canonical-template propagation applies across the six sibling extension-client crates.

Priority P3 because the wasted round-trip is not a correctness bug — it succeeds, just does nothing. But the guard adds defence-in-depth at zero cost and mirrors the pattern used in email_import / email_parse.

Mark says NO HUMAN — clear recommendation, mechanical fix.
